WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:03.730
This voice is a model with four and a half million parameters.

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:07.091
And it was trained without a single recording of a person.

00:00:10.002 --> 00:00:10.874
Hi, I'm Ravel.

00:00:10.874 --> 00:00:14.362
Three text to speech models came out this week, all called small.

00:00:14.362 --> 00:00:16.105
They mean different things by it.

00:00:17.950 --> 00:00:19.329
Start with tiny voca.

00:00:19.329 --> 00:00:23.466
Its waveform begins as a pulse train, one click per vocal fold

00:00:23.466 --> 00:00:25.879
cycle, at the pitch the network predicts.

00:00:26.299 --> 00:00:28.497
Beside it, plain noise, for breath and hiss.

00:00:30.099 --> 00:00:34.089
Every ten point seven milliseconds the network predicts a filter for each

00:00:34.089 --> 00:00:35.418
source, as a cepstrum.

00:00:35.838 --> 00:00:38.792
Filter both, add them, and you have speech.

00:00:38.792 --> 00:00:42.853
The whole synthesizer is twenty-five thousand eight hundred and sixty-two parameters.

00:00:44.767 --> 00:00:48.749
The two voices began as sentences describing them, rendered by a bigger

00:00:48.749 --> 00:00:50.739
model, Qwen three T T S.

00:00:51.139 --> 00:00:53.670
That teacher read every sentence in both voices.

00:00:54.070 --> 00:00:57.309
Whisper checked each clip, and failures were made again or dropped.

00:00:57.709 --> 00:00:59.388
An aligner timed every phoneme.

00:01:00.309 --> 00:01:04.565
Then tiny voca trained from scratch, in under eight hours on one GPU.

00:01:04.565 --> 00:01:06.202
No person in the data.

00:01:06.202 --> 00:01:09.803
But the teacher, Whisper and the aligner all learned from people.

00:01:11.492 --> 00:01:14.669
The whole project cost under ten dollars of GPU time.

00:01:14.669 --> 00:01:16.893
Making the data cost more than training.

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:22.027
Kitten T T S two Mini has Kokoro's exact configuration.

00:01:22.027 --> 00:01:25.753
Phonemes go through a small BERT, then a prosody predictor.

00:01:26.173 --> 00:01:29.341
A voice is just a file, a table of style vectors with

00:01:29.341 --> 00:01:30.661
one row per input length.

00:01:31.081 --> 00:01:32.704
The decoder makes the waveform.

00:01:32.704 --> 00:01:36.274
It is two thirds of the model, stored as int eight,

00:01:36.274 --> 00:01:37.572
eighty-two point seven megabytes.

00:01:39.258 --> 00:01:43.800
Tontaube is two point nine billion parameters in four transformers, one per codebook.

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:46.595
The first decides the words and the timing.

00:01:47.015 --> 00:01:48.994
Three smaller ones add acoustic detail.

00:01:49.789 --> 00:01:51.630
The second refinement is smaller again.

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:55.004
In four bits, each frame still reads every one of them.

00:01:55.424 --> 00:01:56.950
And the third is the smallest.

00:01:58.197 --> 00:02:01.382
About one point two gigabytes of weights per eighty milliseconds.

00:02:01.802 --> 00:02:06.128
Real time needs about fifteen gigabytes a second from memory, before any arithmetic.

00:02:06.128 --> 00:02:08.791
That is the floor, not the whole bill.

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:13.802
Then I ran all three on one old six-core CPU.

00:02:13.802 --> 00:02:18.005
tiny voca made speech over a hundred times faster than real time,

00:02:18.005 --> 00:02:20.806
Kitten about three times, and Tontaube under half.

00:02:20.806 --> 00:02:23.608
On a GPU, Tontaube reached about five times.

00:02:24.850 --> 00:02:26.890
Small can mean parameters, bytes or bits.

00:02:26.890 --> 00:02:28.930
Check which one before you ship it.

00:02:32.650 --> 00:02:35.813
A filtered buzz, Kokoro at one byte a weight, and a big

00:02:35.813 --> 00:02:37.922
model your CPU has to keep up with.

00:02:38.342 --> 00:02:40.373
Every source is in the full article.

00:02:40.373 --> 00:02:40.953
I'm Ravel.

00:02:40.953 --> 00:02:41.243
Bye!
